00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.170
Hello, and welcome back to another video
with ItsDrDan, and today we're going

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.590
to be learning how to draw zwitterions,
which are different structures for

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.369
amino acids, and we're going to be
looking at them in different pHs.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:21.760
So exactly, let's go through it, let's
look at what an amino acid is, and

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.660
try to understand what the basic idea
of a zwitterion is, and some tips and

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:30.370
tricks behind how to draw these for
your introductory biochemistry course.

00:00:30.914 --> 00:00:34.435
So for what we're going to be starting
with is just looking at a normal amino

00:00:34.435 --> 00:00:40.114
acid here and What we got to realize is
that it does not actually exist like this

00:00:40.145 --> 00:00:45.064
when it is in a physiological condition
Meaning when it's in the body because

00:00:45.064 --> 00:00:46.954
your body is full of solutions, right?

00:00:46.955 --> 00:00:49.734
It's essentially we are
one giant bag of liquid.

00:00:50.065 --> 00:00:52.475
So if we were to look at this, right?

00:00:52.475 --> 00:00:56.050
We have a couple different things
we want to label We have our

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.920
carboxylic acid side of the amino
acid, which is our C terminal.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:06.100
We have our amino acid side,
which is on the left hand side,

00:01:06.269 --> 00:01:07.529
so we have our amino acid.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.160
That is known as an N terminal.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.250
We have our R group as well,
which is generally very special

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.170
for every one of the 20 amino
acids that we find in the body.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:23.609
And where that R group is located
is on what we call the alpha carbon.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.760
So every single amino acid occurs in
that order from left to right is an amino

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.750
acid on the left, on that left hand side.

00:01:32.135 --> 00:01:39.005
or the left hand side, and then it
goes NCC every time, right, NCC, NCC.

00:01:39.214 --> 00:01:41.454
As they're bonding in those
proteins, right, it's going

00:01:41.454 --> 00:01:42.905
to make a polypeptide chain.

00:01:44.264 --> 00:01:47.335
Now what does it mean if it's, how
do we look at this in the body?

00:01:47.375 --> 00:01:52.074
Well, if we have this structure
here, what is going to be changing?

00:01:52.615 --> 00:01:55.445
Well, what do acids and bases do?

00:01:55.555 --> 00:01:59.955
Well, to kind of review that a little
bit, right, acids donate a proton.

00:02:00.415 --> 00:02:06.035
So if we have that carboxylic acid side,
That is going to donate a proton, and it's

00:02:06.054 --> 00:02:10.704
going to exist to look a little bit more
like this, where we're going to have a

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:14.914
negative charge on our carboxylate group.

00:02:15.475 --> 00:02:17.045
And where's that hydrogen going to go?

00:02:17.054 --> 00:02:18.565
Well, it needs to go to a base.

00:02:19.095 --> 00:02:21.444
And where, well, how can
we label these two sides?

00:02:21.445 --> 00:02:22.924
Well, if I have my acid side...

00:02:23.415 --> 00:02:28.315
You also have your base side, right,
because amines are your organic bases.

00:02:28.725 --> 00:02:34.725
So when that happens, it will accept
that extra proton, which will then give a

00:02:34.725 --> 00:02:40.105
positive charge on our nitrogen group, a
negative charge on our carboxylic group,

00:02:40.574 --> 00:02:43.635
creating what is known as a zwitter ion.

00:02:44.825 --> 00:02:49.445
Now zwitter ions have two charges, so
one way for you to help remember two

00:02:49.445 --> 00:02:54.335
charges is you think the word zwitter has
two Ts, right, so two Ts, two charges.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:57.560
So that was one way a student
of mine told me to remember that

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.359
and it's, it can stick, right?

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:00.440
Those little things help.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:07.710
So two charges, now as a result, those
two charges are overall neutral, okay?

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:13.630
So when we have that neutral charge,
because the positive cancels out

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:19.480
the negative, that is making an
overall zero charge as a result.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:25.460
So we have a zero charge from that when we
have those two charges combining together.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:27.030
So keep that in mind, right?

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.150
Neutral means zero overall.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.010
Let's go through this and go
through some different examples of

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.980
how we can apply this, and let's
go look at some tips and tricks.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.040
Alright, tips and tricks for these.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:45.180
These are sometimes the most
complicated thing for introductory

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.390
students to draw, because assets and
bases are really hard to understand.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:53.110
So one thing we have to realize is
that the physiological conditions

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:57.550
of your body vary depending
on where we are talking about.

00:03:57.899 --> 00:04:02.079
Every organ has its own unique pH,
which breaks down different types of

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.760
chemicals through metabolism differently.

00:04:05.295 --> 00:04:09.455
Now, as a result, we, one way to
remember how to draw these is think

00:04:09.455 --> 00:04:11.295
of how acids and bases behave.

00:04:11.885 --> 00:04:17.475
Acids donate protons, so, meaning
that they generally give them away.

00:04:18.015 --> 00:04:21.620
So, what I want you to think of is
when we have an acid, Think about

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:26.610
adding H Now what do bases do?

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.710
Bases receive a proton.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.250
So what does that mean that bases do?

00:04:32.459 --> 00:04:35.469
They will remove H plus from something.

00:04:35.989 --> 00:04:41.500
So when we're referring to the
different pHs of the physiological

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:47.359
body, if we are in a basic or a
high pH, we're removing an H plus.

00:04:48.025 --> 00:04:53.305
And if we're in a low pH, then
we are going to be adding H plus.

00:04:53.335 --> 00:04:56.115
Okay, so low and high pH.

00:04:56.665 --> 00:05:02.245
Now what is that all in res in essentially
in respect to, well, if we have, let's

00:05:02.245 --> 00:05:07.075
say, a Witter ion, well, we have to
think of how does the amino acid behave?

00:05:07.075 --> 00:05:08.750
What does it consider a low and high?

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:14.934
Well, every amino acid has what
is known as an isoelectric point.

00:05:15.535 --> 00:05:19.955
Now this is where essentially
it will be a zwitterion.

00:05:20.075 --> 00:05:23.274
Every amino acid has its own unique value.

00:05:23.715 --> 00:05:27.184
Now for the isoelectric point,
generally we'd label that as

00:05:27.954 --> 00:05:30.885
PI whenever we are seeing that.

00:05:30.885 --> 00:05:33.914
So where that zwitterion is
located, like the one that we have

00:05:34.314 --> 00:05:35.744
here in the middle of the screen.

00:05:36.215 --> 00:05:41.185
This is drawn as this ion,
meaning that the body's pH is

00:05:41.245 --> 00:05:43.315
equal to its isoelectric point.

00:05:43.865 --> 00:05:47.545
So how will that structure
change if I'm to go both ways?

00:05:47.605 --> 00:05:49.285
So let's think of those tips from above.

00:05:49.645 --> 00:05:50.935
Remember, add hydrogen.

00:05:51.265 --> 00:05:52.285
Remove hydrogen.

00:05:52.865 --> 00:05:58.425
So if I go, let's say on the,
um, the left hand side here.

00:05:58.425 --> 00:06:00.855
Let's say if I want to be more acidic.

00:06:01.355 --> 00:06:04.975
Well, what exactly is going
to happen to this structure?

00:06:05.504 --> 00:06:06.835
Well, let's think.

00:06:06.854 --> 00:06:14.854
So if I am to be at a lower pH, so
pH less than the PI, meaning I am

00:06:14.854 --> 00:06:18.055
acidic, We are going to add hydrogen.

00:06:18.755 --> 00:06:20.865
So where can you add an H plus?

00:06:20.895 --> 00:06:24.405
What's the thing that most
likely wants to accept an H plus?

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:26.075
The negative area, right?

00:06:26.634 --> 00:06:32.684
So when that happens, right, we're going
to add a hydrogen to that negative area.

00:06:32.734 --> 00:06:34.544
Okay, so we're going
to put that right here.

00:06:35.135 --> 00:06:38.085
Now, as a result, how
does the charge change?

00:06:38.115 --> 00:06:42.065
You can see overall, the
charge is equal to...

00:06:43.815 --> 00:06:45.465
plus one as a result.

00:06:45.465 --> 00:06:49.785
So when that is an acidic condition,
we can see that as a plus one.

00:06:50.505 --> 00:06:54.875
Now, if we want to go the other way,
let's say if I want to go basic in

00:06:54.875 --> 00:07:00.645
this case, well, same idea here, is
we're going to be removing H plus.

00:07:00.854 --> 00:07:04.365
So where can you remove H plus
from, from your zwitterion?

00:07:04.745 --> 00:07:07.325
Where is it the easiest
to take that away from?

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.610
Well, as you can see, we have an
extra one on that amino group.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:13.380
So, what are we going to do?

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:18.909
We're going to remove that, that plus
charge, take away a hydrogen, therefore

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:25.959
making a, um, an ion that is now
able to act like a base by removing.

00:07:26.849 --> 00:07:32.310
When the pH is greater than the PI,
we now have a charge of negative

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.920
one, which is our structure.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:38.630
Okay, so if you remember the acids
and bases, add H plus charge.

00:07:38.945 --> 00:07:41.765
Remove H plus, it's gonna
make it a lot easier.

00:07:42.275 --> 00:07:45.955
So let's take a look at some different
examples and see if we can practice

00:07:45.955 --> 00:07:47.424
this and master this together.

00:07:47.544 --> 00:07:51.325
Okay, so now in the following example,
what are we going to be doing?

00:07:51.895 --> 00:07:58.075
Well, this says draw the structure of
lysine at the following phs and the PI is

00:07:58.075 --> 00:08:03.015
equal to 9.7, meaning that the isoelectric
point for this amino acid that's

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:08.665
drawn here, this is what is considered
to be neutral for that amino acid.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:13.720
So, if I have the amino acid here,
what exactly would it look like if

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:15.770
it was a physiological condition?

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.840
Well, that is where the
zwitterion will come into play.

00:08:19.505 --> 00:08:23.235
So if I am going to have my zwitterion,
the first thing we've got to remember

00:08:23.235 --> 00:08:26.365
is remember acids donate that proton.

00:08:26.895 --> 00:08:32.554
So now when it does that, we are going to
have that negative charge on the O, and

00:08:32.554 --> 00:08:39.424
that hydrogen is going to be added over
to that, um, that H3, and it's going to

00:08:39.424 --> 00:08:42.075
put a positive charge on our nitrogen.

00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:44.830
Where can we go first?

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:47.740
Let's fill in these different structures.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.179
So if I want to go to, let's say,
a higher pH, right, so this is

00:08:53.179 --> 00:08:58.210
considered to be basic conditions,
what would essentially happen?

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:03.160
So I'm at a pH of 12 here, where the pH is
a lot higher than that isoelectric point.

00:09:03.659 --> 00:09:06.180
So base, we're removing hydrogen.

00:09:06.669 --> 00:09:08.729
So where can I remove hydrogen from?

00:09:09.009 --> 00:09:13.100
The only place that is available
is that amino group that is

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.950
on that, uh, side over here.

00:09:16.464 --> 00:09:19.265
So we can remove it from that location.

00:09:19.915 --> 00:09:24.565
So if I do that, right, it takes away
the charge, and as a result, we have

00:09:25.464 --> 00:09:29.944
H2N, and that is a basic amino acid.

00:09:30.665 --> 00:09:31.855
Let's go more acidic.

00:09:31.895 --> 00:09:34.444
What if you start traveling
through the digestive tract

00:09:34.754 --> 00:09:37.014
and things start to change?

00:09:37.505 --> 00:09:42.395
So where, what will we first have
is we are now going more acidic.

00:09:42.895 --> 00:09:46.865
So on these next two, you can see
it gets progressively more acidic.

00:09:47.355 --> 00:09:51.485
Meaning that the farther and farther
you go, it could start changing more

00:09:51.485 --> 00:09:53.025
and more throughout the structure.

00:09:53.624 --> 00:09:57.784
The first place that we are going
to be doing acidic conditions

00:09:58.215 --> 00:10:00.735
is, remember, is adding hydrogen.

00:10:01.634 --> 00:10:03.754
So where can we add hydrogen first?

00:10:03.834 --> 00:10:09.114
Well, right directly to the amino
backbone, where the amino group,

00:10:09.114 --> 00:10:10.854
the carboxylic acid group is.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.410
So we are going to add a hydrogen to the
one place that is possible, and that's

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:18.820
going to be right on that carboxylic acid.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.580
Being that the amino group already
has one, there's no room for

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:24.450
you to add one to that location.

00:10:25.344 --> 00:10:30.344
Now, as this starts getting even
more and more acidic, so if I

00:10:30.385 --> 00:10:35.104
take this thing, is there anywhere
else that we can add a hydrogen?

00:10:35.685 --> 00:10:38.984
And the one thing you're always
looking at, are there any amino

00:10:38.984 --> 00:10:41.164
groups that we can add this to?

00:10:41.625 --> 00:10:45.295
And if you look at the ARD group, all the
way at the bottom, that's on both these

00:10:45.295 --> 00:10:51.495
sides here, This is a basic amino acid,
meaning that eventually, if you keep

00:10:51.495 --> 00:10:57.155
lowering that pH, we will add a hydrogen
down below with another charge down

00:10:57.155 --> 00:11:03.015
here, and that is going to, once again,
add a hydrogen into another location.

00:11:03.584 --> 00:11:08.605
So we can see, as it travels from basic
to what is considered neutral with the

00:11:08.625 --> 00:11:14.469
isoelectric point, all the way to acidic,
the charges are changing as a result.

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:17.480
And we can even write down
the charges here, right?

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:23.269
We see that goes from negative 1
to neutral, positive 1 to positive

00:11:23.269 --> 00:11:28.629
2, uh, overall, as we go from one
structure to the next to the next.

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:30.669
Let's do one more together.

